\f1\b\fs48 \cf2 \uc0\u55357 \u56523  Comprehensive Master Project Requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 You are building a multi-customer vulnerability management platform managed by an operations team. The system consists of a 
\f1\b React JS UI
\f0\b0 , a 
\f1\b Spring Boot backend API
\f0\b0 , and a 
\f1\b PostgreSQL database
\f0\b0 . It operates as a highly flexible ETL pipeline that reads varied security vendor files, maps and transforms their data, and tracks clean operational states.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 \uc0\u55356 \u57303 \u65039  Core Functional Pillars\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 1. Security Software & Source Template Manager\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Software Registry:
\f0\b0  A centralized lookup of standard security products used across your client base (e.g., Kaseya, Rapidfire).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ingestion Layout Rules:
\f0\b0  Each software product supports one or more source templates defining how data arrives.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 File Format Flexibility:
\f0\b0  Native support for parsing 
\f1\b CSV, TSV, PSV, XLS, and XLSX
\f0\b0  files.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Parsing Configurations:
\f0\b0  * A flag to indicate if the first row contains column headers, OR an option to prompt the user to manually verify and define the headers for that upload.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Columns default to a 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 string
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  type unless explicitly mapped or configured otherwise.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Lifecycle Controls:
\f0\b0  Operators can easily enable, disable, or delete software profiles and individual template rules.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 2. Customer Management & Bespoke Configurations\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Customer Registry:
\f0\b0  Stores basic customer/tenant organization metadata.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Product Mapping:
\f0\b0  Configures which security software tools and specific templates are authorized and active for a given customer.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Bespoke Overrides:
\f0\b0  Supports custom, client-specific file layouts. The system can auto-generate a customer-tied template (named dynamically via a pattern like 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 CustomerName_Software
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ) based on either a sample file upload or manual column definition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 3. The Interactive ETL Mapping & Transformation Layer\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 To normalize messy, multi-vendor files into your platform's standard format, both standard software templates and custom templates utilize an interactive mapping wizard:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mix-and-Match Field Mapping:
\f0\b0  The UI allows users to map any incoming column from the uploaded file directly to a destination column in your system's target standard schema, adjusting data type compatibility rules on the fly.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Inline Transformation Functions:
\f0\b0  Users can apply cleaning utilities to incoming source data before it hits the database. Supported functions include:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 TRIM
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  / 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 LTRIM
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  / 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 RTRIM
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Strip leading/trailing whitespace)\
\ls3\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 TO_UPPER
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  / 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 TO_LOWER
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Enforce casing rules)\
\ls3\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 REMOVESPACES
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Strip internal spaces)\
\ls3\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 REPLACE_CHARS
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Swap or strip bad characters/delimiters)\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}set to null\
{\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}set to fixed value string or number\
{\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}floor , roof, correct todecimal etc. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 4. Optimized Ingestion & State Tracking (Single-Table Model)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Single Vulnerability Database:
\f0\b0  All successfully parsed vulnerability findings are stored in a 
\f1\b single, unified database table
\f0\b0  matching your standard target schema.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Decoupled Upload Metadata (
\f0\b0\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 upload_details
\f1\b\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ):
\f0\b0  To avoid massive row-by-row data duplication, metadata is never written directly to individual vulnerability records. Instead, every ingested row points to an 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 upload_id
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 . A dedicated master table tracks the metadata exactly once per run:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 upload_id
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Unique identifier)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 customer_id
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Target client)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 uploaded_by
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (The authenticated user who performed the action)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 uploaded_at
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Timestamp of execution)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 file_name
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (The raw source file name)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 upload_status
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Success, Partial Failure, etc.)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 error_summary
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  / 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 error_count
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (High-level run metrics)\
\ls4\ilvl1
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 error_log_path
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  (Reference path or ID to the download file or error tracking entity)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Active vs. Historical States:
\f0\b0  * The latest successful upload for a customer is marked or determined as the 
\f1\b Active
\f0\b0  data snapshot.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 All older datasets for that customer linked to previous 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 upload_id
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  entries are dynamically treated as 
\f1\b Inactive/Historical/Old
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 5. Validation Pipeline & Error Isolation Loop\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ingestion Integrity Engine:
\f0\b0  During an import, incoming rows are strictly validated against the customer's active template rule, checking for data type compatibility, null constraints, and structural alignment.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Partial Ingestion:
\f0\b0  If a file contains corrupted or invalid rows, the engine processes all valid records into the vulnerability table rather than failing the entire upload.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Downloadable Error Fix Files:
\f0\b0  * In the UI\'92s upload history list view, any run containing failures will feature a 
\f1\b Download Failed Records
\f0\b0  line-item action button.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Clicking this generates a file containing the 
\f1\b exact raw input rows that failed
\f0\b0 .\
\ls5\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The engine 
\f1\b appends a brand new column to the far end of this file
\f0\b0 , providing a clear, explicit line-item explanation of the error for that exact row.\
\ls5\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Users can download this file, immediately identify the breaking lines, correct them inside the file, and re-upload it to complete their data catch-up.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

